const EVENTSIDX={"version":"events-2026.10.1","hash":"3323763b3627dae6","events":[{"id":"great-depression","title":"The 1929 crash and the Great Depression","short":"Great Depression","years":"1929–1939","start":"1929-10","end":"1939","region":"United States and the gold-standard world","kind":"crisis","standfirst":"A stock market crash in October 1929 was followed by three years of collapsing output, prices and banks, and a decade of mass unemployment. The economic question is why a downturn that began like many others became the deepest slump of the industrial era.","question":"How did a stock market crash turn into a decade-long depression, and which policy choices made it deeper or ended it?","subs":["3.1","3.2","3.3","3.5","3.6","4.2","4.5"],"kc":["Change","Interdependence","Intervention","Economic well-being","Equity"],"economists":["keynes","friedman"],"tags":["Smoot–Hawley Tariff Act (1930)","Reconstruction Finance Corporation (1932)","Revenue Act of 1932","Emergency Banking Act (1933)"]},{"id":"bretton-woods-1971","title":"The end of Bretton Woods","short":"End of Bretton Woods","years":"1971–1973","start":"1971-05","end":"1973-03","region":"United States and the world","kind":"policy","standfirst":"For a quarter of a century after 1944, most major currencies were pegged to the US dollar, and the dollar was tied to gold. Between 1971 and 1973 that arrangement came apart, and the main currencies moved to floating exchange rates that still largely govern the world economy.","question":"Why could a system of fixed exchange rates anchored on a gold-convertible dollar not survive the strains of the late 1960s, and what changed when it ended?","subs":["4.5","4.6","3.3","3.5"],"kc":["Change","Interdependence","Intervention"],"economists":["keynes","friedman"],"tags":["Suspension of gold convertibility","Wage and price controls","Import surcharge","Currency revaluation"]},{"id":"stagflation-1970s","title":"Stagflation and the Volcker disinflation","short":"Stagflation","years":"1971–1983","start":"1971-08","end":"1983","region":"United States (with global effects)","kind":"shock","standfirst":"For a decade the United States suffered rising prices and rising unemployment at the same time, a combination many policymakers of the 1960s had treated as unlikely. Ending it required a central bank willing to accept a deep recession, and the episode reshaped how economists think about expectations and credibility.","question":"Why did inflation and unemployment rise together in the 1970s, and what did it cost to bring inflation back down?","subs":["3.2","3.3","3.5"],"kc":["Change","Intervention","Economic well-being","Choice"],"economists":["friedman","keynes"],"tags":["Wage and price controls","Voluntary incomes policy (Whip Inflation Now)","Reserve targeting by the Federal Reserve","Credit controls"]},{"id":"oil-1973","title":"The first oil shock, 1973–74","short":"Oil shock","years":"1973–1974","start":"1973-10","end":"1975-03","region":"Global: oil importers in North America, Western Europe and Japan","kind":"shock","standfirst":"In late 1973 the price of the world’s most important commodity roughly quadrupled within three months. The question for economists was how to respond to a shock that raised inflation and unemployment at the same time.","question":"How can a rise in the price of one input push an economy into inflation and recession at once, and what can policy do about it?","subs":["2.2","2.5","2.11","3.2","3.3","3.5","4.6"],"kc":["Change","Interdependence","Scarcity","Intervention","Economic well-being"],"economists":["friedman","keynes"],"tags":["Emergency Petroleum Allocation Act (1973)","National 55 mph speed limit (1974)","IMF oil facility (1974)","International Energy Agency emergency sharing (1974)"]},{"id":"oil-1979","title":"The second oil shock, 1979–80","short":"Second oil shock","years":"1979–1980","start":"1978-10","end":"1982","region":"Global: Iran, the United States, Western Europe and Japan","kind":"shock","standfirst":"Revolution in Iran took one of the world’s largest oil exporters out of the market, and fear did the rest: crude prices more than doubled. This time the decisive response came from central banks, which chose to break inflation even at the cost of deep recession.","question":"Why did a partial loss of oil supply cause such a large price rise, and why was the policy response so different from 1973?","subs":["3.2","3.3","3.5","3.7","4.5","4.6"],"kc":["Change","Intervention","Economic well-being","Interdependence","Equity"],"economists":["friedman","keynes"],"tags":["Fed reserve targeting (October 1979)","Credit Control Program (1980)","Crude Oil Windfall Profit Tax Act (1980)","Oil price decontrol (1979–81)"]},{"id":"plaza-1985","title":"The Plaza Accord and the falling dollar","short":"Plaza Accord","years":"1985–1987","start":"1985-02","end":"1987-12","region":"United States, Japan and West Germany (G5)","kind":"policy","standfirst":"By early 1985 the US dollar had risen so far that American manufacturers were losing markets and Congress was considering protectionist laws. Five governments then tried something unusual: to talk and trade a major currency down together, and later to hold it steady.","question":"Can governments deliberately move a floating exchange rate, and what happens to trade and asset prices when they do?","subs":["4.5","4.6","3.5","4.3"],"kc":["Interdependence","Intervention","Change"],"economists":["friedman"],"tags":["Coordinated foreign exchange intervention","Exchange-rate target ranges (Louvre Accord)","Discount-rate cuts (Federal Reserve, Bank of Japan)","Lender-of-last-resort liquidity support"]},{"id":"india-1991","title":"India’s 1991 crisis and the turn to reform","short":"India 1991","years":"1990–1994","start":"1990-08","end":"1994-08","region":"India","kind":"crisis","standfirst":"In mid-1991 India’s foreign exchange reserves had fallen so low that the country was close to defaulting on its external payments, and its gold was flown to London as security for a loan. The emergency became the starting point for the most far-reaching change in Indian economic policy since independence.","question":"How did a distant war and years of fiscal deficits push India to the edge of default, and why did the response go beyond rescue to a change of economic model?","subs":["4.6","4.5","3.6","3.7","4.2","4.10"],"kc":["Change","Interdependence","Intervention","Economic well-being","Equity"],"economists":["sen","ricardo"],"tags":["Devaluation of the rupee (July 1991)","Gold pledge to the Bank of England and the Bank of Japan","IMF stand-by arrangement (1991)","New Industrial Policy (1991)"]},{"id":"dotcom-2000","title":"The dot-com bubble and bust","short":"Dot-com","years":"1995–2002","start":"1995-08","end":"2002-10","region":"United States, with global technology markets","kind":"bubble","standfirst":"A genuine technological change, the commercial internet, drew a wave of investment and a rise in share prices that many investors came to see as permanent. The episode asks how a real innovation can still produce an asset bubble, and why its collapse led to only a mild recession.","question":"How can investors overvalue a technology that really does change the economy, and why did the bust hurt shareholders far more than workers?","subs":["3.2","3.5","3.3","2.4"],"kc":["Change","Choice","Efficiency","Intervention"],"economists":["keynes","hayek"],"tags":["Interest-rate changes","Income tax cuts and rebates","Accounting and audit regulation","Margin requirements"]},{"id":"asia-1997","title":"The Asian financial crisis","short":"Asian crisis","years":"1997–98","start":"1997-07","end":"1998-12","region":"East and South-East Asia","kind":"crisis","standfirst":"Some of the fastest-growing economies in the world lost a large share of their dollar income within a year, after a run on the Thai baht spread across the region. The episode asks how a fixed exchange rate, short-term foreign borrowing and fragile banks can turn a currency fall into a deep recession.","question":"Why did a currency devaluation in one middle-sized economy turn into a regional recession, and did the policy response help or hurt?","subs":["4.5","4.6","3.5","3.2"],"kc":["Interdependence","Intervention","Change","Economic well-being"],"economists":["stiglitz","friedman"],"tags":["Exchange-rate peg","Floating exchange rate","Interest-rate increases to defend a currency","IMF conditional lending"]},{"id":"gfc-2008","title":"The global financial crisis","short":"Global financial crisis","years":"2007–09","start":"2007-08","end":"2009-06","region":"United States, Europe and the world economy","kind":"crisis","standfirst":"Losses on US mortgages that were small relative to the world economy set off a run on the financial system and the deepest global recession since the 1930s. The episode asks how a housing bust became a banking panic, and what governments and central banks did to stop it becoming a depression.","question":"How did falling house prices in parts of the United States bring the global financial system close to collapse, and did the policy response work?","subs":["3.2","3.5","3.6","2.10","4.5"],"kc":["Interdependence","Intervention","Economic well-being","Change"],"economists":["keynes","friedman","hayek","stiglitz"],"tags":["Interest-rate cuts","Lender-of-last-resort lending","Quantitative easing","Bank recapitalisation"]},{"id":"eurozone-2010","title":"The euro area sovereign debt crisis","short":"Euro crisis","years":"2009–2015","start":"2009-10","end":"2015-08","region":"Euro area: Greece, Ireland, Portugal, Spain, Italy","kind":"crisis","standfirst":"Between 2009 and 2015, several euro-area governments lost access to borrowing at affordable rates, and their economies went through deep and long recessions. The crisis tested whether a single currency could work without a common budget, a common banking system or a lender of last resort for governments.","question":"Why did a debt problem in one small economy grow into a crisis that threatened the euro itself, and why was recovery in the hardest-hit countries so slow?","subs":["4.4","4.5","3.6","3.5","3.3"],"kc":["Interdependence","Intervention","Equity","Change"],"economists":["keynes","friedman","stiglitz"],"tags":["Conditional rescue loans","Fiscal consolidation (austerity)","Structural reforms","Sovereign debt restructuring"]},{"id":"covid-2020","title":"The COVID-19 economic shock","short":"COVID-19","years":"2020–2023","start":"2020-01","end":"2023","region":"Global","kind":"pandemic","standfirst":"In spring 2020, governments deliberately shut large parts of their economies to slow a virus, producing the sharpest fall in output in post-war records for many countries, followed by an unusually fast rebound. The policy response was larger and faster than in 2008, and economists still debate how far it contributed to the inflation of 2021–23.","question":"What kind of shock was the pandemic, a fall in demand, a fall in supply or both, and did the policy response cushion it at the cost of later inflation?","subs":["3.1","3.2","3.3","3.5","3.6"],"kc":["Change","Intervention","Equity","Economic well-being"],"economists":["keynes","friedman"],"tags":["Lockdowns and social distancing rules","Wage subsidies (furlough schemes)","Direct transfers to households","Government-backed business loans"]}]};
